1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:13,700
A training loss averages over n examples, so its gradient is a sum of n terms. Costly for huge n.

2
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:21,033
Instead draw a random batch of b examples and average those gradients. On average, this is the full gradient.

3
00:00:21,033 --> 00:00:27,333
Six examples have these gradients at one point. Their average, the full gradient, is two thirds.

4
00:00:27,333 --> 00:00:34,000
Now list every batch of two. Their averages scatter widely, from minus two to three and a quarter.

5
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:43,000
Their average over all 15 batches is exactly two thirds. Unbiased, yet five batches would step the wrong way.

6
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:48,300
On a bumpy loss surface, the noise may shake the iterate out of a shallow valley.

7
00:00:48,300 --> 00:00:54,800
That is a possibility, not a guarantee: the same noise can just as well push it toward a worse region.

8
00:00:54,800 --> 00:01:02,933
Far from a solution, a rough direction already helps. Near it, the variance of the estimate is the limit.

9
00:01:02,933 --> 00:01:10,133
Take the overparameterized case again: more parameters than examples, and every example can be fit exactly.

10
00:01:10,133 --> 00:01:17,366
Use one random example per step, starting from zero. Its update is the gradient of that example's squared loss.

11
00:01:17,366 --> 00:01:24,866
Measure the distance to the minimum-norm solution. Since the fit is exact, the error updates alike.

12
00:01:24,866 --> 00:01:32,133
Expand the squared norm of the next error. This identity is exact: a good step subtracts a positive amount.

13
00:01:32,133 --> 00:01:39,266
Replace the per-example factor by its worst case, with a step below one over rho squared: an upper bound.

14
00:01:39,266 --> 00:01:46,466
Average over the random example: each has probability one over n, so the sum becomes the norm of X q.

15
00:01:46,466 --> 00:01:55,800
The error stays in the row space, where X q is at least sigma min times q: a contraction factor alpha below one.

16
00:01:55,800 --> 00:02:06,866
Try two equations, three unknowns: rho squared is 1.25, sigma min squared is 0.75, so alpha is 0.7.

17
00:02:06,866 --> 00:02:15,166
Single runs jitter but all fall. The average of many runs sits just under the bound: a line on a log scale.

18
00:02:15,166 --> 00:02:21,866
The reason: at the interpolating solution every example has zero gradient, so the noise vanishes there.

19
00:02:21,866 --> 00:02:29,566
A special case: it needs exact fit, full row rank and a small step. With noisy labels a constant step stalls.

20
00:02:29,566 --> 00:02:38,633
Strict decrease alone is not enough: a decreasing sequence can stall above zero. The factor alpha does the work.

